        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For most home mechanics the right setup is a half-inch click wrench in the 10 to 150 foot-pound range, plus a three-eighths wrench for the engine bay. The Tekton 24335 and 24320 cover that pair for less than many single professional wrenches. Truck owners should start higher, with a wrench that reaches 250 foot-pounds such as the Precision Instruments split beam or the CDI.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whatever you buy, the habits matter as much as the tool: clean threads, a smooth pull, stop at the click, wind a click wrench down after use, and get it checked every year or two. Do that and a modest wrench will keep your wheels, brakes and engine fasteners where the engineers intended.
